  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nA dominant upper level ridge axis will translate east this \nafternoon into tonight with the arrival of an initial cold front. \nThis ridge will provide hot and dry conditions to NE Montana today\nwith slightly lower temperatures on Sunday as winds turn west to \nnorthwest. Extreme heat headlines continue into Sunday given highs\ntoday near 100 with low overnight recoveries by Sunday morning. \nHot and dry weather will also make for high fire danger so will \nkeep the red flag going into Sunday as well. Finally, as west to \nnorthwest winds ramp up on Sunday, ranging 15 to 25 mph, gusting \nto 35 mph, will maintain the Lake Wind Advisory for Fort Peck \nLake. Smoke and haze will also secondarily create visibility \nreductions at times into tonight, and those sensitive to \nrespiratory symptoms should continue monitoring conditions into \ntonight.\n\nA second cold front will arrive Sunday night into Monday as an\nupper low moves over the region, flattening the departing ridge as\nit moves east. This will usher in cooler temperatures for early \nnext week offering a relief from the heat and fire weather \nconcerns. There may be an isolated to scattered shower or \nthunderstorm Sunday night with this, but otherwise look for mostly\ndry weather into the first part of next week. \n\nLonger term, ensembles begin to show the trough moving downstream\nas a new ridge becomes established over the Pacific Northwest. \nThis will extend height rises over eastern Montana with northwest \nflow aloft and moderating temperature toward seasonable averages \nlate week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nWhile NBM handled the big picture well across the forecast, a\nblend with NBM90 was used for near term winds this weekend in line\nwith the expected windy conditions supporting the Red Flag and\nLake Wind headlines in effect. Pops were also adjusted for Sunday\nevening in collaboration with surrounding offices. Confidence is\nhigh on hot and dry conditions this weekend, but more moderate for\nshowers and thunderstorms on Sunday evening given expected spotty\ncoverage. Having said that, there is a fairly strong emerging\nconsensus among the placement and timing along a southwest to\nnortheast axis Sunday night among the high resolution models.",
